INT. SCHOOL BUS - NIGHT

Empty seats. A single dome light. The bus idles at the curb of a dark football field. Rain streaks the windows.

YASMINE HADDAD, 17, graduation gown over a hoodie, sits alone in the back, knees up, a diploma cover clutched in both hands. She isn't crying. She just can't leave.

At the front, the driver's seat is empty. A phone on the dashboard buzzes. She ignores it.

It buzzes again. She sighs, crawls up the aisle, and answers.

YASMINE
Hello?

MOM (V.O.)
(over the phone)
Habibti, where are you? Everyone's at the restaurant.

YASMINE
I'm on the bus.

MOM (V.O.)
What bus?

YASMINE
The school bus. It's still here.

MOM (V.O.)
Why?

YASMINE
I don't know. I didn't want to go home yet.

MOM (V.O.)
Is something wrong?

YASMINE
No. Nothing.

She sets the phone against her cheek. Footsteps outside. A shape under an umbrella.

COACH TEO ABARA, 40s, the driver and gym teacher, climbs the steps, shakes out his umbrella, and freezes.

COACH
Yasmine?

YASMINE
Coach.

He sets down the umbrella. Rubs his neck.

COACH
I forgot my keys.

YASMINE
They're on the dash.

He picks them up. Doesn't leave.

MOM (V.O.)
Yasmine? Who is that?

YASMINE
Coach Abara, Mama.

MOM (V.O.)
Tell him thank you. Tell him he was very good to you.

YASMINE
(to him)
She says thank you.

COACH
She's welcome.

He sits across the aisle. They listen to rain on the roof.

MOM (V.O.)
Come to the restaurant, habibti. We'll wait.

YASMINE
Okay. Ten minutes.

She hangs up. Puts the phone down. Looks at the empty bus.

YASMINE (CONT'D)
It's smaller than I remembered.

COACH
Everything is.

She almost smiles. Stands. Picks up her diploma cover.

YASMINE
Drive me?

He nods. Takes the wheel. The bus rumbles to life and pulls away.
